## Prerequisites<a id="prerequisites"></a>

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), or [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/) with
  [antsibull-nox](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/antsibull-nox/) in one
  Python environment.
{# skeleton-variant: check-platforms #}
- [Podman](https://podman.io/) for the `ansible-test` sanity sessions and the
  container platforms of Molecule. See
  [`extensions/molecule/README.md`](./extensions/molecule/README.md) for
  virtual machines and the execution environment scenario.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Use an Ansible version matching [`meta/runtime.yml`](./meta/runtime.yml).
Collection dependencies are declared in [`galaxy.yml`](./galaxy.yml).

## Checks<a id="checks"></a>

Run the checks from the collection's Git root. With
[nox installed](#prerequisites), `nox` replaces `uv run noxfile.py`.

{# skeleton-variant: check-commands #}
```bash
uv run noxfile.py                 # format, then run default checks
CI=true uv run noxfile.py         # check only
uv run noxfile.py -l              # list sessions
uv run noxfile.py -s helper-lint  # Python helpers
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule -- test --platform-name molecule-debian13
```
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Among the default sessions:

- `runtime-check` installs the built collection in isolation and keeps only
  what ships: `meta/`, `plugins/`, `roles/` and `playbooks/`. It checks Python
{# skeleton-variant: runtime-scope #}
  imports, module documentation, and role loading and syntax, then runs the
  unit tests against that installation. Molecule covers how the roles deploy.
  To check the exact archive of a [release](#releases):

{# end skeleton-variant #}
  ```sh
  uv run noxfile.py -s runtime-check -- --artifact /path/to/collection.tar.gz
  ```

{# skeleton-variant: deployment-lint #}
- `ansible-lint` runs with foundata's rules from `ansible-lint-foundata`,
  after checking that the rules the configuration enables are active. With
  `nox --no-venv` and the extension missing, it warns and runs only the stock
  rules.
{# end skeleton-variant #}
- `ansible-lint` only reports, with or without `CI=true`; correct its findings
  by hand. Its fix mode is not available: ansible-lint refuses it with this
  project's `.yamllint`, whose brace and comment rules its formatter cannot
  meet, and says so in every run.
- `ansible-lint` also checks the examples: each fenced `yaml` or `yml` block of
  `README.md` and the roles' READMEs that holds a list of plays or tasks, and
  each module's `EXAMPLES`. It lists the other blocks, such as variables or
  inventories, as skipped. A block that is not valid YAML fails with its source
  and line, and so does an option that a task gives one of the collection's own
  modules and that `ansible-doc` does not document. It lints a copy of each
  example in `.nox/ansible-lint/tmp/examples/`, named after its source and the
  line that opens it; a finding's line is the source's. Nothing fixes the
  copies, so correct the source; `# noqa` works in an example as anywhere. With
  `nox --no-venv` the examples are not linted.
{# skeleton-variant: helper-scope #}
- `helper-lint` checks the collection's Python with [ruff.toml](./ruff.toml):
  `plugins/`, `extensions/molecule/`, the roles' `files/`, `tools/`, `scripts/`
  and `tests/` apart from `tests/output/`. Add it to CI configurations that
  list sessions explicitly.
{# end skeleton-variant #}
- `docs` is a default session while a role has `meta/argument_specs.yml`.
  [DocSmith](https://foundata.com/en/projects/ansible-docsmith/) validates the
  argument specs and writes the variable documentation into the marked regions
  of the READMEs and beside the variables in `defaults/`, formatted by rumdl as
  `.ansible-docsmith.toml` configures. Without such a role, the session does
  nothing and says so.

{# skeleton-variant: pytest-runner #}
Put the collection's own pytest suites below `tests/unit/` so `runtime-check`
runs them against the built archive. Other Python below `tests/` is linted by
`helper-lint` but is not run as a test suite.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Session definitions: [noxfile.py](./noxfile.py).
Built-in session settings: [antsibull-nox.toml](./antsibull-nox.toml).
{# skeleton-variant: check-iteration #}
While editing, run the sessions of one ansible-core release, such as
`uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity-2.21` (`-l` lists them), and the
default sessions before a commit.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

The `molecule` session can run from several directories at the same time, such
as a second clone or a Git worktree. Each directory's containers, virtual
machines and Molecule state carry a workspace name of their own, which the
session prints when it starts; see the
[Molecule documentation](./extensions/molecule/README.md). Two runs at once from
one directory are not supported.

Below a deep checkout, nox gives each session a short symbolic link as
`TMPDIR`, because Unix socket paths are limited in length. A test of code that
refuses symbolic links in a path resolves `TMPDIR` first.

Without `TMPDIR`, the sessions use `/var/tmp/nox-<uid>` instead of `/tmp`, which
is often a small tmpfs. Every run shares this directory, and only you may use
it. Nox warns and keeps `/tmp` when the directory has another owner or other
permissions.

{# skeleton-variant: check-platform-selection #}
Review the platform selection in
[`extensions/molecule/default/molecule.yml`](./extensions/molecule/default/molecule.yml)
before running tests. Use the
[testing instructions](./extensions/molecule/README.md#usage-testing) for
individual platforms, the execution-environment scenario, and retaining
instances for debugging. Tests create and remove containers or virtual machines.

{# end skeleton-variant #}
## Troubleshooting<a id="troubleshooting"></a>

To recover a short-path directory left by a killed Nox invocation, find it as
`nox.*` below `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, or below `/var/tmp` without one;
`uv run noxfile.py` leaves two. Read its `owner.json` at the exact path. It
records the path, the creator's host, PID and creation time and the directory's
device and inode. On that host, the invocation ended if no process has the PID
or the process holding it started after the creation time. Also confirm that
none of your processes has the path in its environment or working directory,
because a session's children outlive Nox. Compare the path without following
links. It holds only `owner.json` and numbered links to temporary directories.
Delete those entries by name, keep the link targets, and remove the directory
with `rmdir`. Leave symlinked or replaced paths and uncertain creators for
investigation. Nox itself removes only the unchanged directory its invocation
created.

A killed Nox invocation can also leave `antsibull-nox-*` directories below
`TMPDIR`, by default `/var/tmp/nox-<uid>`. Delete them when no Nox invocation of
yours runs.

{# skeleton-variant: molecule-faq #}
See the [Molecule troubleshooting guide](./extensions/molecule/README.md#faq)
for role-path detection, air-gapped environments, container limits, and VM
networking.

{# end skeleton-variant #}
## Releases<a id="releases"></a>

Each change users notice adds a fragment to `changelogs/fragments/`;
`00-fragment-template.txt` lists the sections. `release` turns the fragments
into `CHANGELOG.rst` and `changelogs/changelog.yaml`, so neither file is edited
by hand.

Releases require `releasing>=7.0.0` for the `release` command. Install the
development requirement in the maintainer's Python environment:

```sh
python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```

`release build` runs the committed revision's Ansible sanity tests before
building the archive. Set `repository` in `.releasing.toml` before the first
release.

Antsibull's configuration is in
[`changelogs/config.yaml`](./changelogs/config.yaml). Release metadata comes
from [`galaxy.yml`](./galaxy.yml) and [`.releasing.toml`](./.releasing.toml).

Maintainers use foundata's
[Ansible collection release procedure](https://github.com/foundata/releasing/blob/main/docs/ansible-collections.md).
It covers release preparation, building from the committed revision, publication
to Ansible Galaxy, and verification. Publishing requires the maintainer's Galaxy
and GitHub credentials.
{# skeleton-variant: release-qualification #}
{# end skeleton-variant #}
